Reject oversized serialized primitive-function names before making a
variable-length C stack buffer.

A SPECIALSXP or BUILTINSXP record contains a length followed by a name from
R_FunTab.  The longest current name is 25 bytes, but a 67-byte fuzz input
claimed 117901063 bytes.  On the harness's 1 GiB R thread, R_CheckStack2()
permits that allocation; the short input then errors during the read, and
ASan reports a write in its stack-unpoisoning path for the longjmp.  Bound
the declared name length to 255 bytes before calculating length + 1 or
allocating the buffer.

Found by OSS-Fuzz: testcase 5160037442715648 (afl_r_unserialize,
ASan dynamic-stack-buffer-overflow).

Upstream status: r-devel/r-svn PR #410, filed 2026-10-26.
Delete this patch once the fix lands in R trunk (ossfuzz.sh will report
it as already-applied in the meantime, which is harmless).

--- a/src/main/serialize.c
+++ b/src/main/serialize.c
@@ -2009,7 +2009,9 @@ static SEXP ReadItem_Recursive (int flags, SEXP ref_table, R_inpstream_t stream)
 	    {
 		/* These are all short strings */
 		length = InInteger(stream);
-		if (length < 0)
+		/* Primitive names come from R_FunTab.  An implausible length from
+		   the stream must not become a large stack allocation. */
+		if (length < 0 || length > 255)
 		    error(_("invalid length"));
 		R_CheckStack2(length+1);
 		char cbuf[length+1];
